'''''Agave fourcroydes''''', commonly known as '''henequen''', is a species of flowering plant in the family [[Asparagaceae]], native to southern [[Mexico]] and [[Guatemala]].<ref name="61980-1"/><ref name=VerlooveSmith2024>{{cite journal |last1=Verloove |first1=F. |last2=Smith |first2=G.F. |title=An update on ''Agave'' and ''Furcraea'' (Agavaceae; Asparagaceae sensu lato) escaped and naturalised in Madeira |journal=Bradleya |date=2024 |volume=42 |pages=186–196 |doi=10.25223/brad.n42.2024.a20}}</ref> It is cultivated, particularly in eastern Mexico, for its [[leaf fiber]], known as ''henequen'' and used to make [[rope]], [[twine]], and other products.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Henequen {{!}} Agave fourcroydes, Mexican fiber, Sisal {{!}} Britannica |url=https://www.britannica.com/plant/henequen |access-date=2025-03-12 |website=www.britannica.com |language=en}}</ref>

Like [[sisal]], ''A. fourcroydes'' is a sterile [[Hybrid (biology)|hybrid]] that is propagated vegetatively rather than from seed. It produces [[Bulb#Bulbil|bulbils]] and [[basal shoot|suckers]], although cultivation generally relies on suckers.

==Description==
''Agave fourcroydes'' can have a trunk as tall as {{cvt|1.7|m|ft}}. It has a [[rosette (botany)|rosette]] of long narrow (lanceolate) leaves about {{cvt|1.2-1.8|m|ft|0}} long and {{cvt|8–13|cm|in|0}} wide. The leaves are gray-green and have widely spaced teeth along their edges and a strong dark brown terminal spine {{cvt|2–2.5|cm|in|1}} long. Numerous offsets are produced so that very large plants may form. The green to yellow flowers are produced in a many-branched [[panicle]] {{cvt|5–6|m|ft|0}} tall. ''A. fourcroydes'' is sterile, so no seeds are produced, but many [[Bulb#Bulbil|bulbils]] appear after flowering.<ref name=IrisIris00>{{Cite book |last1=Irish |first1=Mary |last2=Irish |first2=Gary |date=2000 |contribution=''Agave fourcroydes'' Lemaire |title=Agaves, Yuccas and Related Plants : A Gardener's Guide |pages=122–123 |publication-place=Portland; London |publisher=Timber Press |isbn=978-0-88192-442-8 |name-list-style=amp }}</ref>

==Cultivation==
The leaves of ''Agave fourcroydes'' yield a [[Leaf fiber|fiber]] also called ''henequen'', which is suitable for [[rope]] and [[twine]] but not of as high a quality as [[sisal]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Henequen {{!}} Agave fourcroydes, Mexican fiber, Sisal {{!}} Britannica |url=https://www.britannica.com/plant/henequen |access-date=2025-03-12 |website=www.britannica.com |language=en}}</ref> It has been cultivated for fibre in eastern Mexico for many years, most commonly in [[Yucatán]], [[Veracruz]], and [[Tamaulipas]], and has been grown widely elsewhere, for ornament as well as for fibre.<ref name=IrisIris00/> It is also used to make {{lang|es|licor del henequén}}, a traditional Mexican alcoholic drink.{{cn|date=March 2026}} It may be used as an [[ornamental plant]] in areas where temperatures below {{cvt|-2|C|F|0}} do not occur, as it is frost tender.<ref name=IrisIris00/>

It can be propagated either from its bulbils or from offsets.<ref name=IrisIris00/>

The first person of Spanish descent to document the plant and its usefulness for ropes and other naval utensils was [[José María Lanz]],{{says who|date=March 2026}} a Mexican-born engineer in service of the [[Spanish Navy]], who studied henequen in Yucatán in 1783.{{primary source inline|date=March 2026}}

===Mezcal===
Henequen, like other species of agave, is used in the production of [[mezcal]].{{cn|date=March 2026}}

